Added by the **42nd Amendment (1976)** on the recommendation of the Swaran Singh Committee; there were 10 duties, and an **11th** was added in **2002**. It is the duty of every citizen to, among others:

- abide by the Constitution and respect its ideals, the **National Flag** and the **National Anthem**;
- cherish the noble ideals of the freedom struggle;
- uphold the sovereignty, unity and integrity of India; defend the country when called;
- promote harmony and brotherhood, and renounce practices **derogatory to the dignity of women**;
- value and preserve our composite culture;
- **protect the natural environment** and have compassion for living creatures;
- develop the **scientific temper**, humanism and the spirit of inquiry and reform;
- safeguard **public property** and abjure violence;
- strive towards excellence;
- (11th) for a parent or guardian: provide **opportunities for education** to their child aged **6 to 14**.

Section 8(1) lists exemptions, including information that would:

- prejudicially affect the **sovereignty, security**, strategic or economic interests of India;
- be **forbidden by a court** or breach Parliament's privilege;
- reveal **commercial confidence or trade secrets** harming a third party's competitive position (unless larger public interest);
- be held in a **fiduciary** relationship;
- **endanger life or physical safety** of a person or identify a confidential source;
- **impede an investigation** or prosecution;
- be **Cabinet papers** (until the decision is taken and the matter is complete);
- be **personal information** with no relation to public activity or interest (Section 8(1)(j); the Digital Personal Data Protection Act 2023 amended this clause to broaden protection of personal information).

**Section 8(2)**: information may still be disclosed if **public interest outweighs** the harm. **Section 8(3)**: most exemptions end after **20 years**. **Section 24**: listed intelligence and security organisations are excluded, **except** for information on **corruption and human-rights violations**.

**Partial disclosure** (Section 10): the PIO should remove only the exempt part and give the rest.

Free legal services are available to anyone who is:

- a member of a **Scheduled Caste or Scheduled Tribe**;
- a victim of **trafficking** or **begar**;
- a **woman** or a **child**;
- a person with a **disability** (including mental illness);
- a victim of a **mass disaster, ethnic violence, caste atrocity, flood, drought, earthquake or industrial disaster**;
- an **industrial workman**;
- in **custody** (including in a protective home, juvenile home, or psychiatric hospital);
- a person with **annual income below the limit** set by the State or Central government (limits differ by state and court).

So, for example, **every woman and every child** qualifies, **whatever their income**.

Following Supreme Court judgments (including **Vishwa Jagriti Mission, 2001**, and the 2009 orders after the Raghavan Committee report), the **UGC Regulations on Curbing the Menace of Ragging in Higher Educational Institutions (2009)** define ragging broadly. It includes any act by a student that:

- **teases, treats rudely or handles roughly** a fresher or other student;
- makes them **do something they would not do willingly**, causing shame, torment or embarrassment;
- causes **physical or psychological harm**, fear or apprehension;
- involves **financial extortion** or forcing them to spend money;
- involves **sexual abuse or assault of any kind, stripping, forcing obscene acts or gestures**;
- **exploits** them for academic tasks (like writing seniors' assignments);
- abuses them **online** (cyber-ragging).

Many states also have specific **anti-ragging laws**, and ragging acts are also offences under the **BNS** (hurt, wrongful restraint, criminal intimidation, sexual harassment, abetment of suicide).

Any **unwelcome** act or behaviour (directly or by implication), such as:

- **physical contact and advances**;
- a **demand or request for sexual favours**;
- **sexually coloured remarks**;
- **showing pornography**;
- any other unwelcome **physical, verbal or non-verbal conduct of a sexual nature** (including messages, emails, gestures).

The following circumstances, connected to such behaviour, also amount to harassment: **implied or explicit promise of preferential treatment**, **threat of detrimental treatment** (for example in appraisal), **threat about present or future employment**, **interference with work** or creating an **intimidating, offensive or hostile work environment**, and **humiliating treatment** likely to affect her health or safety.

The test is **'unwelcome'** from the woman's point of view, and the **impact** matters, not just the intent. 'It was only a joke' is not a defence.

Every employer must:

- constitute an **Internal Committee (IC)** at every office or branch with **10 or more** employees;
- have a written **POSH policy**, and **display** the consequences of harassment and the IC members' names and contacts;
- run **awareness programmes** and **orientation** for IC members;
- provide facilities to the IC and help the woman file a criminal complaint if she chooses;
- act on the IC's recommendations and **file an annual report**.

**IC composition**: a **Presiding Officer** who is a **senior woman employee**; at least **two employee members** committed to women's causes or with legal/social-work experience; and **one external member** from an NGO or association familiar with sexual-harassment issues. **At least half** the members must be **women**.

For workplaces with **fewer than 10 employees**, or complaints against the employer, the **Local Committee** set up by the **District Officer** hears complaints.

Employers who fail to comply can be fined (up to ₹50,000, higher on repetition, with possible cancellation of licence). In **2023** the Supreme Court (Aurelio Fernandez) directed governments and bodies to ensure ICs are properly constituted.

Offences committed by a person who is **not** an SC/ST member against an SC/ST member, including:

- forcing a person to eat or drink inedible or obnoxious substances; dumping waste at their home;
- **tonsuring, garlanding with footwear**, parading naked or similar acts of humiliation;
- **wrongful occupation** of their land, or interfering with their land or water rights;
- forcing **begar** or bonded labour; making them do **manual scavenging**;
- **preventing them from voting**, contesting elections or performing duties of office;
- **intentional insult or intimidation with intent to humiliate** in any place **within public view**, and **abusing by caste name** in public view;
- imposing a **social or economic boycott**;
- denying access to public places, water sources, roads or **places of worship**, or preventing a wedding procession (like riding a horse);
- sexual offences, and more serious IPC/BNS offences committed because the victim belongs to an SC/ST.

**Public officials** who wilfully neglect their duties under the Act can themselves be punished (Section 4).

In **NALSA (2014)**, the Supreme Court recognised **transgender persons as a 'third gender'** and held that:

- the right to **self-identify one's gender** is part of personal autonomy and dignity under Articles **14, 15, 16, 19 and 21**;
- discrimination on the ground of **gender identity** is discrimination on the ground of 'sex' under Articles 15 and 16;
- governments should take steps for their welfare, health, education and public employment.

**Gender identity** (one's inner sense of gender) is different from **sexual orientation** (whom one is attracted to).

Section 4 adds **community service** as a form of punishment, for some petty offences, including:

- **theft of property worth less than ₹5,000** by a first-time offender who returns the property or its value (Section 303(2));
- **misconduct in public by a drunken person** (Section 355);
- **defamation** (Section 356(2));
- attempt to commit suicide to compel or restrain a public servant (Section 226);
- a public servant unlawfully engaging in trade (Section 202);
- non-appearance in response to a proclamation (Section 209).

The idea is reformative: work that benefits the community instead of short jail terms.

The UDHR is a **declaration** adopted as a General Assembly resolution, so it is **not a treaty** and was not itself designed to be legally binding. However:

- it inspired **binding treaties** (the two Covenants of 1966 and many more);
- many of its principles are regarded as **customary international law** (for example, the bans on slavery and torture);
- it influenced the **constitutions** of many countries, including the **Fundamental Rights** in **India's Constitution** (drafted at the same time).

India's courts often cite the UDHR when interpreting rights such as dignity and privacy.

The **Protection of Human Rights Act 1993** set up:

- the **National Human Rights Commission (NHRC)** in New Delhi;
- **State Human Rights Commissions (SHRCs)**;
- **Human Rights Courts** in districts for speedy trial of offences involving human-rights violations.

'**Human rights**' under the Act means rights relating to **life, liberty, equality and dignity** guaranteed by the Constitution or embodied in the International Covenants and enforceable by Indian courts.

The NHRC is headed by a **former Chief Justice of India or former Supreme Court judge**, with other members including experts and the chairpersons of national commissions (for SCs, STs, women, minorities, backward classes, children, and the Chief Commissioner for Persons with Disabilities) as deemed members.
